\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



The bases are integers $2\leq k<l$. This is the nontrivial base convention corroborated by the source's explicit equivalence involving $2^n-1$ and $3^n-1$. All gcds are positive greatest common divisors. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/820}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $H(n)$ be the smallest integer $l$ such that there exist $k<l$ with $(k^n-1,l^n-1)=1$.
Is it true that $H(n)=3$ infinitely often? (That is, $(2^n-1,3^n-1)=1$ infinitely often?)
Estimate $H(n)$. Is it true that there exists some constant $c>0$ such that, for all $\epsilon>0$, $ H(n) > \exp(n^{(c-\epsilon)/\log\log n}) $ for infinitely many $n$ and $ H(n) < \exp(n^{(c+\epsilon)/\log\log n}) $ for all large enough $n$?
Does a similar upper bound hold for the smallest $k$ such that $(k^n-1,2^n-1)=1$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Independently verify and publish the lower-bound deduction; settle H(n)=3 infinitely often, prove a matching all-large-n upper bound and constant, and handle the fixed-base variant. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
How large must the smaller pair of bases be to make their nth powers minus one coprime, and how often do bases two and three suffice?

